r for both  local governments state governments and  banks and private investors to invest  into this fund and we can lend that  capital out  on a bridge a basis for small deals  to get them  capital when they need it and ultimately  get out to market  so again the focus at the end of the day  for the type of work that we do is  developing conformity and scale  to bring private pace investors to ohio  and to kentucky and let me for one  second highlight the kentucky piece  because just two weeks ago we were  successful in  working with a team  in kentucky to pass  enabling legislation for pace and i can  tell you back to the theme of our  conversation and webinar today that if  there was not as much data out there as  there is both in other markets and data  we've created in ohio that was shared to  decision makers in kentucky  there's no way that kentucky decision  makers would have been comfortable  enabling this kind of legislation  there was extensive vetting  extensive questions and extensive  concerns that were raised throughout the  process but by virtue of there being  data  and precedent in other states and  private market activity at the level  that there is  we were ultimately  successful in getting kentucky decision  makers to feel confident it was the  right thing to do to now open up  kentucky  to the billion dollar market that is  that is pace so  we're focused on you know kind of both  sides of the river of the ohio river in  our jurisdiction and making sure that  private capital is accessible on a  commercial scale  for energy efficiency and renewable  energy projects  that  concludes my presentation i look forward  to questions  great thanks so much andy um so i am  cognizant of the fact that chris has a  hard stop um at two and there are a few  uh questions that we did  that were postponed to him so we'll put  one question to him and then we'll shift  things back to uh  back to andy that's not too confusing  for everyone  um so it was a question chris from from  actually someone else called chris uh  about your  2.5 megawatt project um and about a ppa  uh so first of all is that  covered by a single ppa  and can you talk a little bit about the  process if so of tying multiple sites  into a single ppa  um and then also a little bit about the  um the sort of um  price in terms of you know cents per  kilowatt hour that you're able to uh to  achieve and the sort of target uh price  that makes a ppa like that actually  pencil out  yeah so  as i mentioned the typical ppa is not a  bunch of little sites just one or two  big solar fields or farms this in fact  is  666 different meters which is production  meters which measures the amount of  solar  so it definitely takes a lot more lag  work a lot more design  but ultimately all the so each meter has  a production meter which we know how  many kilowatt hours we generate  and that um all those combined so every  month we get bills from each of those  and that's how we we time all together  so again with the financing and the  project development  it was  fo it was mirrored after a typical ppa  but the only difference was there's a  lot a lot of different little sites  but everything kind of wraps up into the  same two and a half megawatt about 10  million dollars of investment the same  contract the same lease  site lease purchase agreement or  excuse me not least purchase but the  roof lease agreement between us and the  owner is the same so it's all  all round up into one project  and then as far as the  the rates  depending on what properties we're at we  have different rates whether they're  commercial residential and different  tiers as well  but typically you know around denver we  get about 11 cents a kilowatt hour  um and we we gave like i said we split  the incentive between us and hud so  we generate um revenue from the roof  lease agreement and then  we also get a discounted rate which gets  past the hut so the discount is about  one cent per kilowatt hour for us  um in the kilowatt hour tariff for us is  between 10 and 12 depending on where  you're at but  so that that that one cent goes to hud  and then dha has the site lease  agreement we get the the rest of it  um rush that benefit through the lease  agreement  and depending on i i  i'm not sure if any of you can answer  this too i that delta of  how how this works in terms of what  what's the discount for the electric  rate you need to make it all work i i  think it depends on  each state and each  investor's tax appetite and all that  like it could be four cents it could be  six cents could be two cents but i think  it's a moving target depending on each  project deal  okay great um so next we've got a few  questions for andy uh first we've  actually had a few listeners uh paul  charlie and uh wyatt who were all  wondering um about the source of the  private capital for the gc home energy  loan program  where that was sourced from how you went  about raising it and how sustainable  this source of funds is as the program  grows and reaches greater adoption rates  yes good question  the source of capital three plus years  ago for that program was the greater  cincinnati foundation which is our  family foundation  in in cincinnati and  that's an impact investment for them so  the way that they look at that is  actually uh a divestment of their mutual  funds and some of the other things that  that sit in their endowment and and a  way for them to invest capital with a  return  expectation but in a below market return  with  the i as well on the creation of  social impact that meets their criteria  so they have been a funding partner to  us in the past and now they're also an  investment partner  that sort of capital is far from  limitless  but they continue as many foundations  actually are at a at a local and  national basis uh  to uh invest more cap more of their  capital into this strategy so  the impact investment  marketplace as many of you may know on  the phone uh itself is is growing  significantly um  seeing there to be you know good  economic ways of solving social problems  and certainly uh i think  you know  uh macarthur foundation  rockefeller brothers fund and some  others are among the more prominent  across the country  and  certainly we've been happy with the  local partnership we have with them we  also  see it as a means of being able to raise  additional public capital at a at a at a  zero or certainly a below market return  expectation and when i say below market  i'm typically referring to something  less than  uh something around three percent  now the one thing that i also forgot to  highlight in terms of sustainable  sources of capital  and  and i'm sorry that i forgot to highlight  this because it's a big deal  uh is  is that this is now  from a secondary market financing  standpoint not just a local program in  cincinnati or a local program in  pennsylvania it's in fact  um  underwriting standards that that we  adopted are now in fact part of  wheel which is a national financing  instrument it's an acronym that stands  for warehouse for energy efficiency  lending and this was at the bottom of  one of my slides  but the first wheel secondary market  transaction using the conforming  standards that  we created amongst places in kentucky  and pennsylvania and others are going to  actually be part of the first secondary  market transaction where citigroup is  coming in and providing institutional  capital  so to the point of the question now  that there's been precedent not only in  cincinnati but in a few other states  adopting these similar standards there  is a source of  institutional capital the cost of that  capital is coming in at around nine  percent that's not the cost yet that  everybody wants to see  but it's a reduction of cost of what was  previously about 16 percent  on the institutional capital side  through a  financing program that fannie and  freddie had offered previously and so  we're going from 16 to nine and  hopefully you know those that are on  this team of structuring this deal afc  first and pennsylvania and the  pennsylvania treasury along with  renewable funding  and others are  are optimistic that the institutional  source of cost of capital can get down  to about seven percent so it's getting  there and it's taking a big step here in  the next few weeks when this first  institutional deal closes  great okay we have another uh good  question from heather um  other than pace do you see a trend in do  you see another trend in commercial  finance the smaller projects in the sort  of fifty thousand to one million dollar  range  and as you stated in your discussion  about residential do you find that  commercial contractors need finance  training as well  the i'll start with the second question  and say yes i think particularly i think  what we've seen and i i don't think this  is unique to cincinnati by any stress  but what we've seen is a is a pretty big  gap  in financing in the  small to medium-sized market so really  any energy project less than three  million dollars to a non-investment  grade  uh  client uh because if it's investment  grade and it's over three million  dollars that's where  other types of bonds that's where  performance contracts and other things  have come in that has typically  provided at least a little bit more  ready access to capital  um  so we believe at scale for our market  pace is that  first big focus of ours in really  resolving some of those gaps  um  what other tools that are out there to  resolve some of those gaps as well i  think particularly perhaps for the 13 or  14 states that don't have pace in place  um it's tough i i don't i'm not aware of  anything that's that's really at scale  that doesn't mean by any stretch that  there's not i know some some boutique  financing firms have some  some pretty unique you know kind of  solar tax equity structures and some  other things that work in some states  um  but um  but beyond our focus for pace and that  small to  medium-sized uh  prod you know project size  i don't have a i don't have a great  answer for for what the other solutions  are out there i think i think those that  are out there are either unique to a  particular jurisdiction  and or a particular financial provider  and not necessarily part of a a quote  unquote national  tool or national market movement  great well on the topic of pace and how  you sort of continue to grow that  there's a question from uh  bahara  who was asking about the interaction and  the nature relationships between local  government and gc in the context of  cpace how easy it was to bring local  governments on board  and how that how that relationship  tended to work  yeah i mean i think the biggest thing we  find for local governments is that  why are we here you know what is what is  the energy alliance and non-profit doing  and  for a local government what we're  effectively doing for them is providing  them an economic development service  at no cost  and in fact doing something in  for them that would otherwise cost them  to do  on their own but doing it in a  consistent consistent and conforming way  for the benefit of a broader regional  financing tool  so when we can basically go in and say  we want to do something for you for free  you just have to accept this tool as as  a viable financing tool in your  marketplace pass legislation that gives  us an ability to connect a private  building owner with private capital  there's no hard city dollars that need  to pass through um  you're serving as a conduit and letting  us do all the administrative work so i  think when we boil it down to  um you know  us providing a free economic development  service for a local government that  connects private capital to a private  building  we typically find a good response not a  hundred percent by any stretch but uh  but but typically it's not a pretty good  response  great so we're almost out of time but uh  one final question  which was about the technical  underwriting for the gc pace program  did you develop your own internal staff  expertise or did you outsource that  technical underwriting  we  do a little bit of both and by that i  mean that we know  in our conversations with private bon  baseball investors we know what they're  looking for  um we know they're we have a good sense  for their underwriting criteria and that  gives us a chance to  make sure that we're collecting that  data  on a  potential project that comes through the  cincinnati region we can package it up  and send that financial package off to  that  private bond investor so it it creates  this role that we call a value-added  conduit  we're certainly not a broker we don't  want to be a broker a broker gets us  into all kinds of securities laws and  other things that we don't want to and  don't need to touch but  we can be a robust matchmaker and that's  the role that we've served formally to  date now at the end of the day um the  particular private bond investor whoever  it is like structured finance associates  or clean fund or some of these others  that um that perhaps folks on the phone  have heard about that are more  nationally prominent  even over wells fargo or hannah  armstrong they certainly have their own  underwriters on staff that will you know  thoroughly vet the project  but we're going to help make sure that  they don't see a project that's already  underwater so by by our internal  kind of first pass of underwriting we're  looking for any big glaring issues  uh so that the private bond investors  don't have to invest their time  uh into seeing something that wouldn't  work  however from a formal  underwriting and final stamp it's  certainly incumbent upon the  private investor to apply their own  scrutiny criteria  great well uh we are out of time um  so i would like to uh thank chris and  andy for a couple of fantastic uh  presentations  along with jason and the team at the  cdfa for really making this a great  webinar  uh i'd like to thank all of our  listeners um we had a ton of excellent  questions and uh we simply ran out of  time to  put all of them to the to the presenters  but the contact details of chris and  andy are in the presentation which you  can download in the go to meeting  webinar window  so if you know if you have anything that  you'd like to follow up with them please  feel free to reach out  i'd like to remind everyone that a  recording for today's talk will be made  available through the yale center for  business and the environment website  and i'd like to also remind everyone to  keep an eye out for the next webinar  in this series which will be scheduled  for may 5th  so this is michael pluckett from the  yale center for business and the  environment saying so long from new  haven connecticut
 

